From 59756acece960054dccdefd343079257736178ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Edward Wiles <emmabenzvi@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 23 Jul 2026 03:11:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] Part III.2: shadow-mode measurement -- zero measured
 cache hit opportunity

Adds shadow_stats::Union{Nothing,Dict}=nothing to run_cm_upper_checkpointed
(cm_checkpoint.jl) -- opt-in, additive-only instrumentation; nothing (every
existing call site) is exactly zero behavioral/perf cost. When set, cb_F!/
cb_G! record the same point-fingerprint scheme the design doc's own
outer_point_key uses, classifying every cb_G! call as same-as-last-F
(already free), could-have-hit-cache (the one class Part III.1 identified
as reachable without persistence), or genuine-miss.

Measured on three real, independent D=20/W=80000/L=50 trajectories
(delta=0.1, 1.0, 2.0, 300s each, same problem instance as the real
production campaign) run on cores disjoint from other active work on the
machine (verified via ps/taskset immediately before launch). Result:
ZERO exact-point revisits across all 19 cb_F! calls in all three runs --
every cb_G! call was either already-free (same-as-last-F) or, since there
were no F-repeats, could not possibly have hit a complete-state cache.
Confirms Part III.1's structural prediction (interior-point/barrier
algorithms don't naturally revisit exact prior iterates) with real data.

Per the brief's own instruction ("if the potential hit rate is negligible
... stop and leave the cache as a prototype"), Part IV (conditional
wiring) is correctly NOT attempted this session -- the complete-state
cache is classified PROTOTYPE_ONLY_NEGLIGIBLE_VALUE.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
